.TH AMAZINGBF 1
.SH NAME
AmazingBF \- Brainfuck compiler and interpreter (HIR interpreter, x86_64 native backend)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B AmazingBF
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
AmazingBF compiles or interprets Brainfuck source.
The pipeline is lexer → parser → AST → optimized HIR → LIR, then either HIR interpretation or x86_64 native code generation.
.PP
If
.I FILE
is
.BR \- ,
source is read from standard input.
.PP
The same
.B cargo build
also produces
.B bf-interpreter
and
.BR bf-compiler ,
which behave like
.B AmazingBF
with a fixed run mode (no
.BR \-m / \-\-mode ).
.B bf-compiler
and
.B bf-interpreter
also use the compile target selected at build time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " " \fIMODE\fR
Run mode:
.B dump
(run pipeline, log sizes only),
.B interpret
(default), or
.B compile
(emit a native executable and write
.BR \&.asm / \&.lst
listings beside the output path).
.TP
.BR \-\-target " " \fIx86_64\-linux|x86_64\-windows\fR
Compile target for
.B AmazingBF
when
.B \-\-mode compile
is selected.
The default follows the build target.
Linux targets emit ELF executables; Windows targets emit PE32+ console executables.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Output executable path for compile mode.
Default:
.BR a.out
for Linux targets and
.BR a.exe
for Windows targets.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-opt\-level " " \fI0|1|2|3\fR
Optimization level:
.B 0
(default) fuses consecutive HIR
.B Move / Add
only;
.B 1
adds one HIR pass: fuses runs of increments/decrements and pointer moves,
peephole around clears, recognizes clear and scan loops, simple affine
update loops (such as copy-to-neighbor idioms), and removes empty loops
when the entry cell is provably zero;
.B 2
repeats the
.B 1
-tier pipeline until the HIR reaches a fixed point;
.B 3
uses the same HIR tier as
.B 2
and, in
.B compile
mode only, may emit a tiny
.B exit(0)
target-native executable when there is no output instruction, or fold fixed stdout when there is output but no input (precomputed output sequence).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase log verbosity (repeat up to three times for \-vvv).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Quiet logging.
.TP
.B \-\-interp\-debug
After HIR interpretation, print tape statistics to standard error (initial/final allocation,
visited index span, right-growth from auto-resize, pointer min/max, cumulative left/right move units).
Ignored outside interpret mode.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help (also shown when no
.I FILE
is given).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
AmazingBF tests/cases/1.bf
AmazingBF \-m compile \-\-target x86_64\-linux \-o ./hello_bf tests/cases/1.bf
./hello_bf
cat prog.bf | AmazingBF \-
bf\-interpreter tests/cases/1.bf
bf\-compiler \-o ./hello_bf tests/cases/1.bf
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
If set to a single level token (\fBerror\fR, \fBwarn\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBdebug\fR, \fBtrace\fR, \fBoff\fR), applies that level globally. Values containing `\fB=\fR` or `\fB,\fR` are ignored (no per-module filter strings).
.TP
.B AMAZINGBF_LOG_FORMAT
Set to
.B json
for JSON logs (only when the binary was built with Cargo feature
.BR json-logs ).
.TP
.B AMAZINGBF_LOG_JSON
If set to
.BR 1 ,
enables JSON log format (compatibility switch; same
.B json-logs
build requirement).
.SH SEE ALSO
Project README and
.B cargo run \-\- \-\-help
for the full CLI text.
